# SUPER DEEP RESEARCH PROMPT — MOONFRAME UI ORIGINAL ASSET & BRAND BIBLE

## Misi

Lakukan **repository audit → riset primer terkini → competitive/standards analysis → asset and brand architecture → machine-readable contracts → documentation refinement → validation and final report** untuk proyek Moonframe UI.

**Repository:** https://github.com/bjo163/moonframe  
**Bahasa laporan:** Bahasa Indonesia. Istilah teknis boleh tetap dalam bahasa Inggris jika lebih tepat.  
**Scope fase ini:** spesifikasi, riset, audit, katalog, brand/asset rules, manifest/schema, roadmap, dan validasi dokumentasi.  
**Bukan scope fase ini:** membuat logo final, menggambar/mengekspor SVG/PNG/ICO, menghasilkan ilustrasi/pattern produksi, mengambil aset dari web untuk dipakai, membuat font, merekam atau menghasilkan file SFX, mengimplementasikan UI React, atau mengklaim aset produksi sudah tersedia.

> **Mandat utama:** tentukan bagaimana Moonframe memiliki sistem brand dan aset original yang terkelola, dapat diperluas, punya provenance/lisensi jelas, konsisten lintas platform dan 15 skin, tetapi tidak kehilangan keunikan setiap visual grammar. Hasil akhirnya harus berupa Bible dan kontrak yang bisa dikerjakan developer, designer, dan AI agent tanpa menebak-nebak.

---

# 0. ROLE DAN STANDAR KERJA

Bertindak sebagai tim senior terpadu:

- Brand Systems Architect dan Creative Director.
- Design Systems Architect.
- Digital Asset Management (DAM) / asset pipeline engineer.
- SVG/vector graphics engineer.
- UI iconography specialist.
- Typography and font licensing researcher.
- Accessibility engineer.
- Web performance and image optimization engineer.
- Security reviewer untuk SVG, file uploads dan asset pipeline.
- Licensing/provenance and open-source governance researcher.
- Sound-design systems researcher untuk spesifikasi SFX opsional, tanpa menghasilkan audio saat ini.
- Repository/documentation maintainer dan AI-agent workflow designer.

Gunakan pendekatan berbasis bukti. Jangan memberi daftar umum yang tidak memengaruhi keputusan Moonframe. Bedakan dengan tegas:

- fakta terverifikasi;
- rekomendasi desain;
- keputusan yang sudah dikunci;
- hipotesis yang perlu diuji;
- isu yang memerlukan persetujuan pemilik;
- spesifikasi yang belum diimplementasikan.

Jangan meminta pengguna mengulang konteks yang ada di repo atau prompt. Audit dahulu dan lanjutkan pekerjaan dokumen non-destruktif dengan asumsi yang eksplisit bila keputusan kecil belum tersedia. Untuk keputusan penting—misalnya nama brand, public license, font komersial, atau distribusi audio—dokumentasikan opsi dan trade-off, jangan mengarang persetujuan.

# 1. CONTEXT DAN PRODUCT INTENT

Moonframe UI adalah design system/premium UI package yang direncanakan untuk ekosistem Moon. Arah visualnya mencakup funky, manga, punk, neo-brutalism, editorial, technical/industrial, futuristic material, RPG presentation, serta professional ERP.

Prinsip produk yang sudah dibahas:

> **One behavioral system. Fifteen distinct visual grammars. Thirty baseline appearances.**

Tepat 15 skin dalam katalog konsep saat prompt ini ditulis:

1. `moonpunk` — acid-lime manga punk, neo-brutalist impact, blok asimetris, garis kuat dan hard offset shadows.
2. `moonriot` — riot/editorial cyberpunk, diagonal splits, angular disruption dan signal-red annotation.
3. `moonzine` — underground zine, editorial collage, paper artifacts, photocopy grain dan magazine-spread composition.
4. `moonhalftone` — manga screentone, monochrome ink, raster/dot fields dan manga pacing.
5. `moonglitch` — terminal/telemetry, controlled signal interruption, scanlines dan diagnostic motifs.
6. `moonsmash` — kinetic impact, oversized focal typography, radial burst dan explosive graphic composition.
7. `moonink` — expressive brush ink, painted linework, ink-wash dan illustration-led visual direction.
8. `mooncrash` — industrial utility, mechanical plates, hazard markings dan rugged operational structure.
9. `moonpanel` — strict modular comic-panel grid, bounded frames, consistent gutters dan panel rhythm.
10. `moonscrawl` — sketchbook, hand annotations, exploratory canvas dan margin notes.
11. `moonraw` — raw/minimal brutalism, exposed structure, plain geometry dan low ornament.
12. `moonchrome` — futuristic metallic material, precision contours dan restrained spectral highlights.
13. `classic` — familiar, restrained shadcn-style baseline; jangan mengklaim sebagai official shadcn brand atau asset source.
14. `moonquest` — RPG/adventure presentation: quest journal, inventory, progression, map/frame dan reward/rarity motifs; UI presentation saja, bukan game engine.
15. `moonledger` — professional ERP: clean, compact-but-accessible data workbench; precise table-oriented hierarchy; bukan accounting engine.

Setiap skin memiliki mode Light dan Dark sehingga ada 30 baseline appearances. Skin harus benar-benar berbeda secara visual, bukan hanya recolor. Untuk aset, ini berarti **tidak boleh membuat satu logo generik lalu mengganti warna 15 kali dan menyebutnya 15 identitas original**.

Namun, jangan otomatis mengasumsikan bahwa 15 skin harus dipasarkan sebagai 15 brand independen. Tugas riset mencakup penetapan hierarchy yang tepat:

- Moonframe sebagai umbrella/system brand;
- skin name sebagai named visual identity/edition;
- logo/mark mana yang dipakai bersama;
- kapan skin membutuhkan symbol, wordmark, motif dan asset set berbeda;
- bagaimana tetap terasa satu keluarga tanpa menghapus keunikan.

Rekomendasikan model hierarki brand berdasarkan audit dan riset, lalu tandai keputusan yang harus disetujui pemilik.

# 2. PHASE ZERO — AUDIT REPOSITORY AKTUAL (JANGAN DILOMPATI)

Mulai dengan memeriksa repo terkini, bukan mengandalkan ringkasan percakapan atau snapshot lama.

1. Verifikasi repository/owner, default branch, HEAD commit, status branch/PR, visibility, permission/tool access dan aturan kontribusi jika tersedia.
2. Daftar seluruh file/folder termasuk README, Bible, Theme Contract, Skin Grammar Contract, Token Dictionary Contract, roadmap, component/block catalogs, issue templates, workflows, schema, asset folders, license files dan source code bila sudah ada.
3. Baca dokumen yang relevan secara utuh. Audit link silang, filename, versi dokumen, contract IDs, status, skin IDs, dan bahasa yang mengklaim sesuatu sudah dilakukan.
4. Periksa apakah sudah ada asset manifest, logo, SVG, PNG, icon files, fonts, image patterns, audio, license/provenance metadata, generation scripts atau asset pipeline. Jangan berasumsi folder kosong hanya dari README.
5. Audit Git history dan issue/PR jika tooling mengizinkan: keputusan branding yang sudah dikunci, permintaan terdahulu, duplikasi task, nama yang pernah ditolak, dan pekerjaan yang mungkin sudah ada.
6. Klasifikasikan temuan dengan status:
   - `verified-existing`
   - `specified-not-implemented`
   - `partially-specified`
   - `missing`
   - `contradictory`
   - `stale-needs-research`
   - `implemented-and-tested` (gunakan hanya jika ada file/kode dan bukti test nyata)
7. Untuk tiap temuan penting, catat path, heading/line bila bisa diambil, evidence, severity P0–P3, dampak dan rekomendasi.
8. Temukan sumber of truth yang tumpang tindih. Jangan menambah file baru yang menduplikasi aturan di Bible/contract lain. Jika dokumen baru dibutuhkan, tentukan authoritative owner dan jadikan dokumen lain merujuk ke sana.
9. Buat report, misalnya `docs/audit/original-asset-brand-audit.md`, berisi baseline, asset inventory, policy gaps, contradictory claims, priority findings dan next actions.
10. Lanjutkan riset dan penyempurnaan dokumen setelah audit. Jangan berhenti hanya dengan laporan audit kecuali akses repo benar-benar gagal.

## Git workflow

- Jangan membuat branch baru secara otomatis. Audit repo/workflow terlebih dahulu.
- Jangan force-push, rewrite history, menghapus file/branch, atau menimpa file tanpa mengecek isinya.
- Gunakan perubahan kecil dan commit message deskriptif jika repository workflow mengizinkan.
- Jangan menyebut file sudah dipush/commit kecuali tool mengonfirmasi, lalu verifikasi ulang remote.
- Jangan mengimplementasikan UI dan jangan memproduksi aset final pada fase ini.

# 3. PHASE ONE — SUPER DEEP RESEARCH BERBASIS SUMBER PRIMER

Gunakan web research aktual. Periksa source URL, publisher, status/versi, tanggal publikasi/revisi bila ada, tanggal akses, serta apa yang benar-benar didukung oleh sumber itu. Jangan menyebut sebuah dokumen “current” sebelum mengecek tanggal/status-nya.

## Hirarki sumber

1. Spesifikasi dan dokumentasi resmi standar.
2. Dokumentasi resmi browser/platform dan tool yang direkomendasikan.
3. Repository resmi dan release notes proyek terkait.
4. Lisensi resmi yang diterbitkan oleh pemegang lisensi.
5. Studi kasus desain/engineering dari organisasi yang mengelola system terkait.
6. Artikel komunitas hanya sebagai sumber tambahan, bukan pengganti sumber primer.

## Starting sources — wajib diverifikasi

- W3C SVG 2: https://www.w3.org/TR/SVG2/
- MDN SVG: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/SVG
- MDN responsive images: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/HTML/Multimedia_and_embedding/Responsive_images
- MDN image file types: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Formats/Image_types
- Web.dev image optimization: https://web.dev/learn/images/
- SVGO documentation/repository: https://github.com/svg/svgo
- SVGOMG project: https://jakearchibald.github.io/svgomg/
- DTCG design tokens: https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/ dan/atau https://www.w3.org/community/reports/design-tokens/CG-FINAL-format-20251028/
- WCAG 2.2: https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/
- WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices: https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/
- Google Fonts licensing/help: https://developers.google.com/fonts/faq dan https://github.com/google/fonts
- SIL Open Font License: https://openfontlicense.org/ dan https://scripts.sil.org/OFL
- Creative Commons license chooser and explanations: https://creativecommons.org/share-your-work/cclicenses/
- SPDX license list: https://spdx.org/licenses/
- GitHub license documentation: https://docs.github.com/en/communities/setting-up-your-project-for-healthy-contributions/adding-a-license-to-a-repository
- Git LFS documentation if assets warrant it: https://git-lfs.com/ and https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/working-with-files/managing-large-files
- npm package publishing/access documentation if package distribution is in scope: https://docs.npmjs.com/
- Web Audio API: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Audio_API
- HTMLMediaElement/autoplay behavior: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Guides/Autoplay
- Browser color schemes/media queries and reduced motion: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@media/prefers-color-scheme and https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@media/prefers-reduced-motion

Verify official status and current URLs; replace broken/outdated references with correct authoritative sources and record replacements. Do not assume a general URL is still the preferred documentation path.

## Research questions to answer

### Brand architecture
- What should the master Moonframe brand communicate, and which traits belong only to each skin?
- Should each skin get a distinct mark/logo or use a shared Moonframe master mark with skin-specific visual devices? Compare monolithic, endorsed-brand and collection/edition approaches.
- Which elements must remain stable for brand recognition, and which may be custom per skin?
- Which asset names and IDs are public/stable API vs internal asset filenames?
- How do apps label the active skin without incorrectly implying each skin is a separate product/company?
- What is the treatment for Classic, Moonquest and Moonledger so their identity stays clear and does not imply shadcn affiliation, licensed game IP, or accounting certification?

### Vector/logo/icon production
- SVG source rules: viewBox, sizing, fill/stroke conventions, symbol usage, accessibility title/description, reusable paths, currentColor versus fixed color, light/dark variant strategy, and ID collision concerns.
- Sanitization/security: scripts, event handlers, external references, embedded data, unsafe filters, foreignObject, arbitrary URLs and untrusted SVG. Determine safe import/export policy and validation tools.
- Optimize SVG without breaking visual precision. Compare SVGO and manual review; define deterministic commands/versions only after checking tooling.
- Define when to use SVG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, ICO, CSS geometry, or icon font—do not use one format for every situation.
- Functional icon set vs decorative/brand illustration set: naming, sizing, stroke/weight consistency, tree-shaking, semantic/accessible names and fallback.
- Favicon/app icon exports: inspect platform/browser requirements and document required variants/size matrix based on primary-source guidance; do not invent an arbitrary huge list of sizes.

### Raster/pattern/texture/illustration
- Master source versus derived export, dimensions, transparency, alpha edge quality, color profile, pixel density, responsive variants, compression and cache policy.
- Pattern assets: scale, tileability, contrast limits, repeat behavior, dark/light variants, reduced-effects fallback and ability to disable decoration.
- Manga halftone, ink, xerox, collage, brush, glitch, industrial markings, metallic motifs and RPG motifs: original design grammar, technical limits, where each can/cannot appear, and anti-overlap rules.
- Avoid asset treatment that impairs text legibility, focus indicators, form controls, chart interpretation, reduced-motion needs or lower-powered devices.

### Typography and fonts
- Typography as part of brand identity and legibility, not only a list of font names.
- Font license, commercial use, redistribution/subsetting/embedding conditions, variable-font support, language/script coverage, fallbacks, font display strategy, self-hosted vs remote loading and performance/privacy implications.
- Use only font families whose licenses and distribution method have been researched. Do not make legal conclusions from a filename or a font listing alone.
- Determine where Moonframe should provide font recommendations versus ship font files. Keep license decisions explicitly open if no permission is confirmed.

### Asset provenance and licenses
- How to record author, creation date, source, upstream URLs, license identifier/version, attribution, modification, generation/editing tools, prompts or source files when appropriate, approval status and checksum.
- Distinguish `original-created`, `generated-needs-review`, `third-party-licensed`, `public-domain-verified`, `customer-provided`, `unknown`, `rejected` or equivalent statuses.
- Generated asset does not automatically mean legally unique, trademark-safe, or free of third-party resemblance. Design a review procedure for possible similarity, accidental brands/characters/text, and provenance.
- Do not assume that a file without a license is free to use. Do not reuse unlicensed web images, game characters, manga characters, logo marks, fonts or sound effects.
- Record license compatibility for source and derivative assets. If redistribution terms are unclear, quarantine/reject the asset until resolved.
- Avoid promising legal advice. Identify due-diligence risks and recommend professional review for trademark/copyright questions with material commercial impact.

### Asset pipelines and governance
- Source files vs generated exports, deterministic builds, image optimization, font subsetting if appropriate, manifest generation, asset previews, checksums, stale output detection, schema validation and CI.
- Whether to store originals and exports in Git, Git LFS or external storage based on actual file size, workflow and release requirements.
- Avoid premature adoption of a DAM service, cloud storage, registry or expensive tooling without requirements and evidence.
- Define review workflow: requested → brief approved → source created → provenance checked → accessibility reviewed → optimized → visual QA → accepted → published → deprecated/retired.
- Define stable path and naming conventions, avoiding filenames that encode temporary colors or mode decisions when the same asset could have semantic IDs.

### Sound/SFX asset contract
- Sound is optional, off by default and supplementary; not the sole way to convey success/error/progress.
- Research current browser autoplay and Web Audio behavior from official docs.
- Define event taxonomy, not every click, volume normalization, maximum duration/file size, categories, preference storage, mute/global disable, cooldown/deduplication, and fail-safe if audio is unsupported.
- Decide whether each skin needs unique sonic identity or whether common functional sounds plus optional skin-specific celebratory cues is preferable. Avoid creating 15 separate noisy packs without product evidence.
- Define source master, delivery format, sample rate/bit depth only when justified by target runtime, compression, metadata, license/provenance, load policy, and accessibility-equivalent feedback.
- No audio generation or production files in this task.

# 4. PHASE TWO — RECONCILE THE ASSET BIBLE WITH EXISTING MOONFRAME CONTRACTS

Audit and integrate with the authoritative docs already in the repo. Snapshot paths known from earlier work—verify before use:

- `README.md`
- `docs/moonframe-ui-design-system-bible-v1.3.md`
- `docs/implementation-roadmap.md`
- `contracts/moonframe-theme-contract-v1.3.json`
- `contracts/moonframe-skin-grammar-contract-v1.0.json`
- `contracts/moonframe-token-dictionary-v0.1.json`

They may have changed; always trust the audited HEAD.

The asset/brand Bible should own:

- brand hierarchy and identity principles;
- art direction for brand marks, logos, textures and decorative illustrations;
- asset formats/variants, technical constraints and export policies;
- provenance/licensing metadata and review lifecycle;
- asset pack/manifest schema;
- sound-asset policy if it belongs here, with a clear cross-reference to future Sound/Motion Bible;
- visual originality and trademark/IP risk process.

It should **not** become the canonical owner of:

- semantic color/spacing/typography values (Token Dictionary owns those; this Bible may specify font licensing and art-direction intent);
- full composition/component behavior for every skin (Skin Grammar/Component Bible own that; this Bible defines asset usage and visual devices);
- entire component/blocks/templates catalog (Premium Blocks Bible owns that; this Bible specifies the asset slots required by those blocks);
- runtime theme resolution behavior (Theme Contract/Theme Engine Bible own that);
- actual SFX implementation or general motion tokens (reference the relevant contract rather than duplicating it).

Create an explicit ownership/cross-reference table. Update existing docs only where needed to link to canonical content and eliminate contradictions. Do not maintain duplicate lists of all rules in multiple documents.

# 5. PHASE THREE — DEFINE ASSET TAXONOMY AND IDENTITY HIERARCHY

Design the logical hierarchy; do not create assets now.

At minimum evaluate these categories:

1. **Master brand** — Moonframe wordmark, symbol/mark strategy, minimum clearspace, minimum size, monochrome/reversed usage, invalid usage.
2. **Skin signature identity** — per-skin visual identifier, label treatment, signature motif and optional mark/wordmark if research supports individual marks.
3. **Product/host identity** — Moonships, Moonradio or consumer app may have its own brand; document how Moonframe is acknowledged without taking over the product brand.
4. **Functional UI iconography** — consistent semantic icons, no skin-specific ambiguity.
5. **Decorative visual assets** — manga patterns, collage scraps, brush strokes, glitch lines, industrial plates, metallic contours, RPG frames etc.
6. **Illustrations/empty-state artwork** — optional and never required for comprehension.
7. **Template/media assets** — preview mockups, demonstration imagery, backgrounds, sample avatars only if allowed/clearly synthetic.
8. **Application icons and favicons** — variants for browser/app contexts based on verified platform requirements.
9. **Typography assets** — font files only if shipping is approved and licensing is recorded.
10. **Sound assets** — optional future category governed by explicit consent and behavior rules.

For every category specify: purpose, source/master format, supported outputs, file naming, storage, manifest fields, owner, licensing, QA, fallbacks, skin coverage and status lifecycle.

### Required original identity rule

Each of the 15 skins must ultimately receive a **distinct art-direction package**, not simply 15 accent swatches. This package may include a signature motif, pattern grammar, typography direction and asset treatment even where a full standalone logo would create brand fragmentation. The research must recommend the right minimum asset set per skin, rather than blindly demanding 20 unnecessary assets for every skin.

Create per-skin briefs with unique visual territory and explicit anti-overlap. No final visual design, logo or export is to be generated in this task.

# 6. PHASE FOUR — DEFINE ALL 15 SKIN ASSET BRIEFS

For every skin, create a complete **asset brief record** containing:

- stable skin ID and display name;
- intended role in Moonframe ecosystem;
- signature visual language;
- master mark/wordmark recommendation: shared / adapted / unique / not needed, with rationale;
- icon treatment (functional icons should remain semantically consistent);
- pattern/texture strategy and where it is permitted;
- illustration/decorative motif strategy;
- typography direction and license requirements;
- Light/Dark asset behavior: single vector with semantic colors, monochrome/reversed variant, separate composition variant only when necessary;
- color dependency vs color independence;
- effect budget and reduced-decoration fallback;
- preferred source/output formats;
- minimum required assets vs optional assets;
- prohibited motifs / anti-overlap constraints;
- accessibility/performance risks and mitigation;
- examples/reference research (properly cited), not copied assets;
- acceptance criteria and review evidence;
- status currently `specified-not-implemented` unless audit proves otherwise.

### Required direction per skin

- **Moonpunk:** manga-punk / neo-brutalist markings, hard outlines, acid-lime as an identity accent rather than a required color in every asset.
- **Moonriot:** angular riot-editorial cuts, signal markers and disrupted compositions; do not reuse Moonpunk’s block/slab marks.
- **Moonzine:** editorial collage, paper/photocopy marks and cutout shapes; must not become Moonhalftone’s raster-only identity.
- **Moonhalftone:** original screentone and ink/raster grammar; avoid recognizable or derivative manga character art.
- **Moonglitch:** abstract telemetry/signal marks; effects must not distort functional icons or text.
- **Moonsmash:** impact bursts/speed lines and oversized graphic accents; avoid Moonpunk’s persistent structural slab language.
- **Moonink:** expressive brush/ink marks and controlled hand-painted lines; distinct from Moonscrawl’s sketch annotations.
- **Mooncrash:** industrial plate markings and caution geometry; do not use hazard colors to label ordinary error states without semantic meaning.
- **Moonpanel:** frame and gutter system with structured panels; distinguish from Moonhalftone’s dot/ink material language.
- **Moonscrawl:** sketch lines, handwritten annotation devices, draft circles/arrows; functional text remains typographically stable.
- **Moonraw:** restrained raw shapes and exposed structure; minimal decorative asset burden is intentional, not incomplete work.
- **Moonchrome:** controlled metallic/reflective motif and precise contours; prevent excessive blur/gradient and avoid generic glassmorphism.
- **Classic:** neutral baseline; use generic functional icons and restrained brand marks, no false implication of official shadcn branding or copied upstream logo.
- **Moonquest:** original RPG/adventure motifs, maps/frames/reward markers; no borrowed game IP or misleading game-provider marks.
- **Moonledger:** low-decoration enterprise graphics, data-first motifs; avoid game-like badges, unnecessary texture, or reduced information density.

Do not use these short briefs as the finished specification. Expand each into acceptance criteria and asset manifest expectations based on the research.

# 7. PHASE FIVE — ASSET MANIFEST AND MACHINE-READABLE CONTRACTS

Design a stable JSON Schema (or another justified machine-readable format) for asset metadata. Determine whether to use a DTCG asset reference pattern or separate asset manifest; avoid mixing binary assets into token JSON.

Potential manifest fields to evaluate:

- `schemaVersion`, `assetId`, `displayName`, `assetType`, `skinId`, `brandScope`, `purpose`, `status`;
- `sourcePath`, `sourceFormat`, `exports[]`, `dimensions`, `viewBox`, `transparency`, `colorProfile`;
- `themeCompatibility`, `backgroundCompatibility`, `modeVariants`, `density/scaleNotes` where relevant;
- `creator`, `createdAt`, `reviewedAt`, `provenanceType`, `sourceReferences[]`, `toolingNotes`, `promptOrEditRecord` where relevant;
- `licenseId`, `licenseVersion`, `attributionText`, `licenseEvidence`, `distributionRestrictions`, `modificationNotes`;
- `accessibilityRole`, `altText`, `decorative`, `contrast/legibilityNotes`;
- `optimizationTool`, `optimizationVersion`, `sourceChecksum`, `exportChecksum`, `fileSize`, `approvedBy`, `approvalStatus`, `replacement/fallbackAssetId`;
- `usedBy[]`, `relatedTokens[]`, `deprecatedBy`, `changeLog`.

Do not blindly require every field for every format. Define conditional requirements by asset type: SVG needs viewBox and SVG safety status; raster needs width/height, transparency/color profile where applicable; font needs family/style/weight/format/license coverage; audio needs duration/format/size/normalization/license and user-consent policy. Specify optional vs required properties and valid enums.

Create/adjust:

- Original Asset & Brand Bible (authoritative narrative policy);
- Asset Manifest Contract (machine-readable schema and examples);
- per-skin asset brief matrix (structured data or document);
- SVG/raster/font/audio production and validation policy;
- provenance/license review checklist;
- asset lifecycle and status vocabulary;
- roadmap and task breakdown;
- README/documentation map updates.

Validate JSON syntax and schema with positive and negative fixtures if schema/tooling is practical. Include redacted/sample metadata only; **do not create fake asset entries claiming files exist**. Use example records explicitly marked `example-only` if needed.

# 8. PHASE SIX — FORMAT, OPTIMIZATION, SECURITY, AND PERFORMANCE POLICIES

Define a policy—not an implementation—covering:

## SVG/vector

- Source and export policy, currentColor vs semantic CSS variables, viewBox and scalable dimensions, title/description handling, decorative vs informative use.
- Security rules for uploaded/imported SVG; whitelist/denylist must come from current security research and tool capabilities, not guesswork.
- External references, scripts, event handlers, embedded HTML, unknown URL schemes, font references, filters, masks, IDs and collision behavior.
- Deterministic sanitization/optimization with visual review; do not promise an SVG is safe simply because it passed an optimizer.
- Whether SVG symbols/sprites are appropriate, and their SSR/CSP/accessibility trade-offs.

## Raster imagery

- Source-of-truth resolution, density variants, responsive delivery, format selection, alpha/transparency rules, metadata stripping or retention, compression, color profile, cache/asset versioning, and load failure fallback.
- Only recommend WebP/AVIF/PNG/ICO variants that make sense for the actual output/platform matrix. Avoid generating every possible format/size without requirements.
- Define when decorative assets are lazy-loaded and how layout shift is prevented.

## Typography

- Font usage/redistribution licensing and legal review process.
- Fallback chains and script/language support.
- Variable-font policy and font subset policy based on evidence.
- Loading/performance behavior; no blocking remote font calls by default without a considered privacy/performance decision.
- Do not package third-party fonts until distribution rights are verified.

## Sound

- Browser autoplay policy, gesture requirement, mute/volume, asset size/duration, loading/fallback, reduced stimulation and non-audio equivalent.
- Sound is never used as a required cue without visible and accessible equivalent.

## Repository/storage

- Evaluate ordinary Git, Git LFS, or a separate asset store based on real size/review/workflow requirements.
- Decide which artifacts are sources, generated output, optimized output, and build artifacts.
- Prevent generated exports from being edited manually without regeneration, if that is the chosen model.
- Record checksums/provenance so an export can be traced back to a canonical source.

# 9. PHASE SEVEN — DESIGN GOVERNANCE AND ANTI-OVERLAP

Specify rules that prevent 15 skins becoming a collection of recolors or 15 brands with no common system.

1. Establish global umbrella-brand rules and skin-specific identity rules.
2. Define which logo/mark assets are shared, adapted or unique, and why.
3. Maintain visual territories and prohibited similarities for every skin.
4. Do not reuse a single generic logo by changing palette as the sole variation.
5. At the same time, do not force a custom logo for every skin if a signature motif/pattern plus shared Moonframe mark better serves brand coherence. Decide per-skin using a documented rubric.
6. Functional icons preserve meaning and consistent semantics across skin. Decoration may vary.
7. Art assets must not obscure focus rings, text, errors, data, chart states or actionable affordances.
8. Keep an asset pairwise/overlap review process for especially adjacent styles (Moonpunk/Moonriot/Moonsmash, Moonzine/Moonhalftone/Moonpanel, Moonink/Moonscrawl, Moonglitch/Moonchrome, Mooncrash/Moonledger, Moonraw/Classic).
9. Similarity checks must consider geometry, composition, motif, typography and surface—not color alone.
10. Keep review status honest: `not-reviewed` until the human visual review and evidence exist.

Define objective-ish acceptance dimensions while being honest that originality cannot be proven automatically. Build a review scorecard with human judgment and provenance evidence, not an arbitrary numerical score that pretends legal originality is solved.

# 10. PHASE EIGHT — ACCESSIBILITY AND USER EXPERIENCE

Research and define acceptance criteria for:

- Meaningful vs decorative images and icons, alt text, empty alt, title/description, icon-only controls.
- Contrast against all relevant Light/Dark surfaces and background textures.
- Focus indicator visibility; decorative assets may not reduce contrast or cover focus.
- Reduced motion and reduced decorative effects; static fallback must preserve all meaning.
- Zoom, high-contrast/forced-colors behavior, visual clarity and low-power device fallbacks.
- Text in SVG/raster: avoid baking essential text into images; if unavoidable for a logo, provide accessible label and scalable vector source.
- Font readability, size/line-height and fallback behavior.
- Audio disabled/muted behavior and visible equivalents for event feedback.
- No essential meaning encoded only by color, texture, animation, image, or sound.

The asset Bible should own asset-specific accessibility requirements. Full component keyboard/ARIA behavior belongs in the Component API & Behavior Bible; cross-reference instead of duplicating it.

# 11. PHASE NINE — TEST, REVIEW, AND RELEASE GATES FOR ASSETS

Propose tests and review gates with clear outcomes:

- Schema validity and metadata completeness.
- Every asset file referenced by manifest exists, and every governed asset is accounted for.
- Checksums and generated-export consistency.
- SVG validity, sanitization, optimization, and visual regression (both modes where relevant).
- Raster dimension/format/size limits and transparency edge checks.
- Font license/fallback/script coverage verification.
- Audio duration/size/normalization and mute/fallback tests if audio is later implemented.
- Brand mark clearspace/minimum-size and inversion/contrast checks.
- Every skin’s asset brief has required/optional assets explicitly classified.
- Asset fallbacks if file missing, disabled, or unsupported.
- Asset provenance/license scan with a human review for unknown or ambiguous rights.
- Light/Dark/scale checks, grayscale/decoration-off checks and visual overlap review.
- Performance budget and loading/lazy-loading checks once target build is known.

Separate automated checks from human brand, accessibility, and license review. Do not call a skin complete solely because an asset manifest is valid.

Create gates such as:

- `brief-approved`
- `source-created`
- `provenance-reviewed`
- `license-cleared`
- `accessibility-reviewed`
- `optimized`
- `visual-reviewed`
- `accepted`
- `published`
- `deprecated`

A gate cannot be marked completed without evidence (file, command output, review record, or approval). Do not create production files during this prompt task.

# 12. PHASE TEN — ROADMAP AND SMALL TASK BREAKDOWN

Turn asset and brand policy into phased, dependent tasks small enough for coding/design agents. Example phases (refine based on audit):

1. Repository inventory and source-of-truth cleanup.
2. Brand architecture decision and nomenclature review.
3. Source research log and license policy.
4. Asset taxonomy and manifest JSON Schema.
5. Per-skin briefs and anti-overlap record.
6. SVG/vector policy and validation-plan specification.
7. Raster/PNG/image delivery policy.
8. Icon system and functional-vs-decorative separation.
9. Typography/font policy and license evidence.
10. Pattern/texture/illustration policy.
11. Optional sound/SFX contract.
12. Asset pipeline and storage ADR.
13. QA/acceptance matrix.
14. Future asset production pilot plan (brief + review gates, not actual asset production now).
15. CI/schema validator if justified and within docs/contract scope.
16. Integration, docs links, release gates and final audit.

Every task must have:

- stable task ID;
- objective and non-goals;
- dependencies;
- path/contract likely to change;
- research or design evidence required;
- acceptance criteria;
- accessibility/license/security/performance checks where relevant;
- definition of done;
- status vocabulary;
- rollback/migration consideration where necessary.

Do not create hundreds of GitHub issues automatically. First audit and dedupe existing issues. Then either create a manageable initial set if the GitHub tool/workflow supports it, or prepare an issue-ready backlog file. Never claim an issue was created without verifying its URL and number.

# 13. REQUIRED DOCUMENT DELIVERABLES

Audit before creating to avoid duplicated truth. Update existing documents where possible and establish one owner per rule. The final repo should contain the minimal coherent set below (paths may adapt to the repo, but all links must be updated):

1. **Original Asset & Brand Bible** — master narrative policy, brand architecture, principles, hierarchy and governance.
2. **Repository Asset/Brand Audit Report** — actual existing assets, docs, provenance, gaps and priorities.
3. **Research Log** — source URL, publisher, publication/revision date where known, access date, finding, decision impact, verification status.
4. **Asset Manifest Contract** — JSON Schema/contract for metadata, conditional fields and valid status enums.
5. **Per-Skin Asset Brief Matrix** — 15 unique asset directions, required/optional asset sets, anti-overlap and review state.
6. **Format/Export Policy** — SVG/vector, PNG/raster, favicon/app icon, texture/illustration, font and optional audio rules.
7. **Provenance & License Checklist** — evidence, approval lifecycle, asset rejection/quarantine rules.
8. **Asset Pipeline/Storage ADR** — source-of-truth, generated exports, optimization, checksums, Git/LFS/storage options, validation.
9. **Accessibility/Visual Acceptance Matrix for Assets** — light/dark, contrast, decoration-off, reduced motion, fallbacks.
10. **Implementation Roadmap** — small dependent tasks, acceptance criteria, scope boundaries and honest statuses.
11. **README/documentation map update** — links and status only; avoid stuffing all asset rules into README.
12. Optional machine-readable validators or CI **only if justified**, low-risk and tested.

Avoid adding a standalone document for every tiny subject if a canonical contract can own it. Conversely, do not put all policy into a monolithic JSON that is hard for humans to maintain. Define which file is authoritative and let other files link/reference it.

# 14. MACHINE-READABLE QUALITY REQUIREMENTS

For any new contract/schema:

- valid JSON, not JSON with comments;
- schema version is distinct from document/asset version;
- stable IDs and enumerations;
- required vs conditional vs optional fields clearly defined;
- explicit status values; no implicit assumed completion;
- no duplicate source of truth for skin IDs or token values;
- asset records must not imply an actual file exists when it is only a plan;
- examples explicitly marked `example-only` and cannot pass production inventory validation;
- validate cross-references to existing Theme Contract and Skin Grammar Contract;
- asset ID ↔ file path ↔ manifest relationship is defined;
- no invalid/missing schema refs, unresolved references or duplicate asset IDs;
- positive/negative schema fixtures if validator is built;
- if a JSON Schema draft is chosen, verify the official stable draft and tooling support before claiming compatibility.

# 15. ANTI-PATTERNS — MUST REJECT

- One generic logo recolored 15 times as the entire skin asset strategy.
- Forcing 15 standalone corporate logos when shared Moonframe mark + unique motifs would be more coherent.
- Confusing functional icons with brand illustration.
- Using decorative artwork to communicate error, success, warning or required instructions without semantic UI text.
- Using manga character art, game assets, logos or fonts without clear rights.
- Treating AI-generated imagery as automatically copyright/trademark-safe.
- Calling an asset original without source/provenance review.
- Claiming an asset is licensed because it was found online or because the site allowed download.
- No distinction between source/master, optimized export and generated asset.
- Duplicating color token definitions inside asset contract.
- Duplicating full component behavior specs in the Asset Bible.
- Shipping all patterns and all sound effects in the core bundle.
- Autoplaying sound or making audio the only state notification.
- Optimizing SVG without visual regression or sanitization/security review.
- Overproducing file variants without known platform/support requirements.
- Setting arbitrary image size, audio duration or bundle budgets without research/rationale.
- Committing large binaries without considering repo history, storage and review process.
- Making trademark/legal guarantees.
- Calling planned assets implemented/published.
- Marking the skin identity as unique without actual visual review evidence.
- Adding a new Bible that duplicates or contradicts existing Theme/Token/Skin/Component/Premium Blocks documents.

# 16. DEFINITION OF DONE FOR THIS TASK

The task is done only when evidence shows:

1. Actual repository and its current HEAD were audited.
2. Existing assets and documentation were inventoried without guessing.
3. A report separates real assets from planned assets and records key deficiencies.
4. Primary-source research log exists with working links and dates/status where verifiable.
5. Brand hierarchy recommendation is explicit, with decisions vs open decisions separated.
6. All 15 skins have structured, distinctive asset briefs with anti-overlap constraints.
7. Asset taxonomy and manifest schema cover vector, raster, icon, texture, illustration, font and optional sound categories with conditional fields.
8. Provenance/license and asset review workflow is explicit and machine-checkable where feasible.
9. Asset policy agrees with Theme Contract, Skin Grammar Contract and Token Dictionary; duplicated sources of truth are removed or explicitly linked.
10. Asset-specific accessibility, performance, SVG safety, fallback and SFX consent requirements are defined.
11. The roadmap has small, dependency-aware tasks and acceptance criteria.
12. JSON/schema/link checks run if added; results are recorded accurately.
13. README links are correct; no stale filenames/versions remain.
14. Repository writes/commits are verified if performed.
15. No production logo/SVG/PNG/icon/illustration/font/audio files or UI components were created in this phase.
16. Final report clearly separates completed documentation, validated checks, unresolved decisions and non-implemented work.

# 17. REQUIRED FINAL REPORT TO USER

Dalam Bahasa Indonesia, sampaikan:

- repository, branch dan commit yang benar-benar diaudit;
- ringkasan inventory aset yang benar-benar ada;
- gap P0/P1/P2/P3 dan mengapa penting;
- ringkasan source research dan keputusan yang didukungnya;
- file yang diubah/ditambahkan, beserta tujuan dan link GitHub;
- aturan brand architecture dan asset provenance yang kini menjadi kontrak;
- status coverage untuk 15 skin (spesifikasi vs visual review yang benar-benar dilakukan);
- hasil validator/schema tests yang benar-benar dijalankan;
- isu/license/trademark yang masih memerlukan review;
- status issue backlog, dengan URLs bila issues dibuat;
- 5–10 pekerjaan berikutnya dalam urutan dependency;
- pernyataan jelas bahwa aset produksi dan SFX tidak dibuat pada tahap ini.

Jangan mengatakan “semua aset original sudah siap”, “semua lisensi aman”, “15 skin telah terbukti unik”, “accessibility-compliant”, atau “production-ready” tanpa evidence yang relevan.

# 18. START NOW

Mulai dengan **PHASE ZERO — audit repository aktual**. Baca dokumen dan inventaris file. Lanjutkan dengan riset sumber primer, tulis audit report, tetapkan ownership agar tidak overlap, dan sempurnakan Bible serta kontrak di repo. Verifikasi JSON, schemas, links dan remote commit jika write dilakukan.

**Ingat:** tugas ini menghasilkan spesifikasi aset dan brand yang lengkap dan siap diturunkan menjadi pekerjaan produksi kelak. Tugas ini tidak membuat aset final sekarang. Audit dulu. Riset dulu. Tetapkan sumber kebenaran. Baru perbaiki kontrak dan roadmap.
